\section{One record per event}{

Records belong to the same earthquake when their EventID.USGS is equal;
legacy processed indexes name that column usgsEventId. A record without
it is identified by its EventID. With oneRecordPerEvent TRUE, selection.csv
keeps one record per earthquake, the one with the largest PGA; ties go to
the larger StationID and then to the smaller RecordID. candidates.csv keeps
every record that meets a criterion. The events count in summary.json
uses the same identity.
}

\section{Event magnitude}{

Before the criteria are applied, EventMagnitude takes a moment magnitude
where one is published or an Ms or mb is converted. When EventMagnitudeType
is mw, mwc, mww, mwr or mwb in any letter case, the magnitude and its type
are kept. Otherwise, when EventMagnitudeType.owner is one of those types,
EventMagnitude.owner is taken and the type becomes Mw. Otherwise an Ms in
any letter case becomes \code{0.67 Ms + 2.13} and its type becomes Mw.
This is the ISC-GEM catalogue relation of Storchak et al. (2013) as
reproduced by \enc{Villalón}{Villalon}-Semanat and Palau-Clares (2018,
equation 10); it is published for \code{Ms <= 6.47}, and the same line is
extrapolated to every larger Ms.
An mb in any letter case without a published Mw becomes \code{0.85 mb + 1.03} for
\code{3.5 <= mb & mb <= 6.2}, the global empirical relation of
Scordilis (2006, equation 22). Outside that interval its value is kept,
so that \code{Mw = mb}; the type becomes Mw in both cases.
An ML without a published Mw keeps its value and takes the type Mw, an identity
meant for the moderate range. Md and other or empty types keep their
magnitude and type. The EventMagnitude and EventMagnitudeType criteria and
the published tables use the resulting values. The source values remain
in the .owner and .USGS columns of the index (and .ISC where present).
}

